  "description": "Kafka cluster health, topic throughput and consumer-group lag for the event-driven-arch scenario.",
  "editable": true,
  "graphTooltip": 1,
  "links": [],
  "panels": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "title": "How Kafka moves a message",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "prometheus"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 5,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0
      },
      "type": "text",
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "A **producer** appends records to a **topic**, which is split into **partitions** \u2014 the unit of parallelism and ordering. Each record gets a monotonically increasing **offset** within its partition.\n\nA **consumer group** reads those partitions and commits the offset it has processed. **Lag = latest offset written \u2212 offset committed by the group.** Lag grows when producers outrun consumers, and it drains when you add consumers (up to one per partition \u2014 a 4th consumer on a 3-partition topic sits idle).\n\nBroker panels come from the JMX exporter on the brokers; lag and offset panels come from kafka-exporter."
      }
    },
